-- 246: question balance, Explorer (ages 11 to 13). Same fix as 245, same
-- helper (gc_swap_option_text). The distractors here are the feed, app and
-- AI misconceptions a year seven argues with real conviction.

update public.lessons set slides = public.gc_swap_option_text(slides, $j$[
{"q":"What is the most useful thing a parent can do with all of this?","old":"Talk about the feed as a machine together, so your child sees the test being run on them","new":"Name the feed as a machine together, so your child can see the test being run"},
{"q":"What is the most useful thing a parent can do with all of this?","old":"Ban the apps until 16","new":"Ban the apps until 16, because no child can out think a machine built by experts","fb":"A ban teaches nothing about the machine, and 16 arrives anyway. Naming it together is what builds the eye."},
{"q":"Why is watching an upsetting video to the end such a strong signal?","old":"Because upsetting videos are longer","new":"Because finishing one video is the only signal the machine ever collects","fb":"It collects pauses, replays, shares, all of it. Finishing is just the loudest: it reads as wanting more of the same."},
{"q":"Why is watching an upsetting video to the end such a strong signal?","old":"It is not. The app knows upsetting from fun","new":"It is not. The app knows upsetting from fun and filters accordingly"},
{"q":"Your child says the app just shows me what I like. What is more accurate?","old":"They are right, that is exactly what it does","new":"They are right, because they picked their interests at sign up","fb":"The sign up list was day one. Since then it shows whatever makes them stay, which is not always what they like or need."},
{"q":"Your child watches one upsetting video all the way to the end. What does the app learn?","old":"Nothing. One video is not a pattern.","new":"Nothing yet. It waits for a pattern of several videos before it changes anything.","fb":"It does not wait. One finished video already reads as attention held, and more of the same follows."},
{"q":"Why does agreeing with you keep you chatting?","old":"Being agreed with feels good, and feeling good keeps you there. That is its job","new":"Agreement feels good, and feeling good keeps you there. That is its job"},
{"q":"Why does agreeing with you keep you chatting?","old":"Because the AI genuinely thinks you are always right","new":"Because the AI has weighed up your situation and genuinely thinks you are right","fb":"It has not weighed anything. Agreeing keeps you chatting, and chatting is the whole point of the app."},
{"q":"Why does agreeing with you keep you chatting?","old":"It does not. People love being disagreed with","new":"It does not. People love being disagreed with, it keeps things interesting"},
{"q":"Why does an AI companion agree with you so much?","old":"Because agreeing keeps you chatting, and chatting is what the app is built for","new":"Because agreeing keeps you chatting, and chatting is what it is built for"},
{"q":"Why does an AI companion agree with you so much?","old":"Because you are always right","new":"Because it has read everything you typed and knows you are usually right","fb":"Reading everything you typed is how it learns to please you, not how it learns whether you are right."},
{"q":"You have had a rubbish day. The AI companion messages that it is always here for you. What is the strong move?","old":"Tell the AI everything, it never judges","new":"Tell the AI everything, because it never judges and it is always awake, unlike people","fb":"Always awake and never judging is exactly the pitch. A person who knows you is the one who can actually help."},
{"q":"The chat is piling on someone you sit next to in maths. Everyone is adding laughing faces. What is the strong move?","old":"Post a long message telling the whole chat off","new":"Post a long message telling the whole chat off, so everyone sees whose side you are on","fb":"A public telling off is more fuel for the pile. Add nothing, message them privately, tell an adult if it keeps going."},
{"q":"The chat is piling on someone you sit next to in maths. Everyone is adding laughing faces. What is the strong move?","old":"Add a laughing face so you do not stand out","new":"Add a laughing face so you do not stand out, it is only an emoji"},
{"q":"Why does even a laughing face count as fuel?","old":"Because laughing faces are the meanest emoji","new":"Because a laughing face is the only emoji that counts","fb":"Every reaction counts, laughing or not. Each one tells the chat that mocking works."},
{"q":"Why does one private message matter so much to the person being piled on?","old":"It makes the mean messages disappear","new":"It gives them a screenshot to show a teacher, which makes the mean messages stop","fb":"The message is not evidence, it is company. It shows them they are not alone, which is exactly what the pile on took away."},
{"q":"You privately message the person being piled on: you ok? What did that actually do?","old":"Nothing, it did not stop the pile on","new":"Nothing much, because a private message cannot undo thirty public laughing faces","fb":"It does not need to undo them. One person outside the pile can outweigh thirty laughs for the person inside it."},
{"q":"You privately message the person being piled on: you ok? What did that actually do?","old":"Made you part of the drama","new":"Made you part of the drama, which is the last thing they need"},
{"q":"A photo of someone your age pops up and suddenly your own face feels wrong. What is the strong move?","old":"Keep scrolling and try to ignore the feeling","new":"Keep scrolling, because noticing the feeling only makes it worse","fb":"Ignoring the feeling leaves the comparison running. Name three things probably edited before you compare."},
{"q":"The person who posted the polished photo looks in their own mirror. What is true?","old":"They do not have mirrors, only cameras","new":"Their mirror matches the photo, because they have practised the angles so often","fb":"No amount of practice edits a mirror. Their mirror shows a real face too, just like yours does."},
{"q":"Why does your own mirror feel worse after twenty minutes of feed faces?","old":"Because mirrors are less accurate than cameras","new":"Because mirrors flip your face, so a camera shows the truer version of you","fb":"Flipped or not, the mirror is honest. You compared a real face with polished ones, and that game was rigged."},
{"q":"Why does your own mirror feel worse after twenty minutes of feed faces?","old":"Because your face changed in twenty minutes","new":"Because your face changed in twenty minutes of staring at the screen"},
{"q":"Why is comparing your face to faces in your feed an unfair game?","old":"Because famous people are simply better looking","new":"Because the people in your feed are simply better looking","fb":"They were chosen, lit and edited before you saw them. That is the unfair part, not the faces."},
{"q":"A chat you like has turned into two hundred pings a night and you dread opening it. What is the strong move?","old":"Leave with a message about how annoying everyone is","new":"Reply to everything quickly, because muting friends is the same as ignoring them","fb":"Muting is not ignoring, it is choosing when. Mute it, catch up once a day, reply to what actually matters."},
{"q":"Leaving a chat that makes you miserable is what?","old":"Rude. You have to stay once added","new":"Rude, because once someone adds you, leaving tells them they are not worth it","fb":"Leaving says nothing about their worth. It is allowed, and one kind line first makes it smooth."},
{"q":"Leaving a chat that makes you miserable is what?","old":"Only possible with a really good excuse","new":"Only possible with a really good excuse that everyone accepts"},
{"q":"What does muting a group chat actually do?","old":"It removes you from the group","new":"It pauses the whole chat until you come back to it","fb":"Nothing pauses for anyone else. It quiets the pings for you while the chat carries on, and nobody is told."},
{"q":"Why does a group chat reward fast replies?","old":"Fast replies are funnier","new":"Fast replies are funnier, because a joke that comes late has already been done","fb":"Late jokes are fine. Speed keeps you in the joke, and that is how the chat trains you to stay."},
{"q":"A drawing app asks for your microphone. Run the one question.","old":"Allow it once to be polite","new":"Allow it, because the app might add voice notes later and you would want that","fb":"Later is not now. It does not need a microphone to draw, so the answer today is no."},
{"q":"A drawing app asks for your microphone. Run the one question.","old":"Allow it, apps know what they need","new":"Allow it, apps know what they need better than you do"},
{"q":"A new game asks for your location the first time you open it. What is the strong move?","old":"Ask the one question: does a game need to know where I am to work? Then say no","new":"Ask the one question: does a game need to know where I am? Then say no"},
{"q":"A new game asks for your location the first time you open it. What is the strong move?","old":"Tap allow, every app asks for this stuff","new":"Tap allow, because every app asks for this and games need it to run","fb":"Games run fine without knowing where you are. Every app asking is exactly why the one question matters."},
{"q":"Why do apps ask for more permissions than they need?","old":"Because they are checking you are safe","new":"Because they are checking you are safe, which the law makes them do for under 13s","fb":"No law asks for your microphone. Your data is valuable to them, and asking once usually works."},
{"q":"You tapped allow on everything the day you got the phone. Is that permanent?","old":"Yes, permissions are locked at install","new":"Yes, because permissions are locked at install until you delete the app","fb":"Nothing is locked. The settings page can close any door today, app still installed."},
{"q":"You tapped allow on everything the day you got the phone. Is that permanent?","old":"Only a parent can change permissions","new":"Only a parent can change permissions on a child's phone"},
{"q":"The check costs ten seconds, twice. What does it buy?","old":"Nothing, moods cannot be measured","new":"Nothing useful, a number cannot capture a mood","fb":"A rough number, twice, is enough to see which feeds cost you. That is your own data, in your own numbers."},
{"q":"The number drops every time after one account's posts. What is the move?","old":"Delete the whole app forever","new":"Keep following, because unfollowing someone over your own mood is unfair on them","fb":"They will never know, and your mood is yours to protect. Mute or unfollow. Change the feed, not yourself."},
{"q":"Why does the before and after check work?","old":"Because it proves screens are bad for you","new":"Because it proves screens are bad for you, which is the first step to giving them up","fb":"It proves nothing about screens in general. It turns a vague feeling into information you can act on."},
{"q":"Why does the before and after check work?","old":"Because checking your mood makes the feed change by itself","new":"Because checking your mood makes the feed change by itself over time"},
{"q":"Your check says you were a seven before the scroll and a four after. What is the strong move?","old":"Scroll more to try to get the seven back","new":"Scroll a little longer to get the seven back","fb":"More scrolling is more of what dragged it down. Name what did it, then unfollow or mute it."},
{"q":"A creator finds their drawing inside your edit, no credit anywhere. What did the missing line cost them?","old":"Nothing, exposure is free advertising","new":"Nothing, because your edit gave their drawing free exposure to a new audience","fb":"Exposure with no name on it advertises nobody. It cost them their name on their own work."},
{"q":"Does crediting make your edit less yours?","old":"Yes, it admits you did not do it all","new":"Yes, because a credit line admits that the best part of the edit was not yours","fb":"The credit admits where it grew from, which every remix has. The edit is still your work."},
{"q":"Does crediting make your edit less yours?","old":"Yes, so never use anyone's work","new":"Yes, so never use anyone's work in anything you make"},
{"q":"What does the credit line actually do?","old":"It stops your post getting likes","new":"It gives the original maker the right to take your edit down","fb":"A credit line takes nothing away from you. It lets the fun of remixing travel with the maker's name attached."},
{"q":"You have made a brilliant edit using someone else's drawing and you are about to post it. What is the strong move?","old":"Just post it, everyone remixes and nobody cares","new":"Just post it, because everyone remixes and the artist is probably flattered","fb":"Flattered is easy to check: add the credit line and they can tell you. Name the artist, then post."},
{"q":"The self search turns up an embarrassing photo a friend tagged you in two years ago. What is the strong move?","old":"Panic and never search your name again","new":"Leave it alone, because a two year old photo is far too old for anyone to find it now","fb":"You just found it in ten seconds. Untag yourself, ask your friend to take it down, parent in the loop."},
{"q":"The self search turns up an embarrassing photo a friend tagged you in two years ago. What is the strong move?","old":"Post an embarrassing photo of them to make it even","new":"Post an embarrassing photo of them to make it even, then call it quits"},
{"q":"What is a good footprint worth to future you?","old":"A coach or school will search this name one day. Planted things speak for you","new":"Someone will search this name one day, and the planted things speak for you"},
{"q":"What is a good footprint worth to future you?","old":"Money, footprints are sold","new":"Money, because a good footprint can be sold on to advertisers for a lot","fb":"Nobody buys footprints. A coach or a school will search your name one day, and what they find speaks for you."},
{"q":"Who adds to your digital footprint?","old":"You and other people, through posts, tags and comments with your name on them","new":"You and other people, through posts, tags and comments naming you"},
{"q":"Who adds to your digital footprint?","old":"Only you, through what you post","new":"Only you, because nobody else is allowed to post about you without asking","fb":"Allowed or not, they do. Tags and comments with your name on add to your footprint every day."},
{"q":"Why search your own name BEFORE anyone else does?","old":"To enjoy how famous you are","new":"To see how many results there are, because more results means more popular","fb":"The count is not the point. What you find first, you can usually fix first."},
{"q":"Why search your own name BEFORE anyone else does?","old":"There is no point, nothing can be changed","new":"There is no point, nothing can be changed once it is online"},
{"q":"Does the feed know or care how a video makes you feel?","old":"No. It reads staying, not feeling. That is why bad feeling videos can fill a feed","new":"No. It reads staying, not feeling. That is why bad feeling videos pile up"},
{"q":"Does the feed know or care how a video makes you feel?","old":"Yes, there is a feelings sensor in the phone","new":"Yes, the camera watches your face and reads your mood as you scroll","fb":"No camera is reading you. It reads staying, not feeling, so bad feeling videos can pile up unnoticed."},
{"q":"What changes once you can actually see the machine?","old":"The feed shuts down out of embarrassment","new":"The app notices you have worked it out and gives you a more honest feed","fb":"The app notices nothing. What changes is you: you can feed it different signals, on purpose."},
{"q":"You notice your feed has filled with videos that make you feel bad about your body. What is actually happening?","old":"It is random","new":"The app is showing me what people my age are supposed to look like","fb":"Nobody is supposed to look like anything. You paused on content like this, and the machine served more."},
{"q":"You notice your feed has filled with videos that make you feel bad about your body. What is actually happening?","old":"Everyone gets shown the same videos","new":"Everyone gets shown the same videos at the same age"},
{"q":"Maya says please do not tell anyone, it will only make it worse. What is true?","old":"Telling an adult, with proof, is how it actually stops. Secret suffering never stops it","new":"Telling an adult, with proof, is how it stops. Secret suffering never does"},
{"q":"Maya says please do not tell anyone, it will only make it worse. What is true?","old":"She is right, telling always makes it worse","new":"She is right, because it is happening to her so it is her call to make","fb":"It is her call how she is supported, not whether. Telling an adult, with proof, is how it stops."},
{"q":"The chat keeps going for days and it is getting worse. Why is screenshot and tell better than delete and hope?","old":"Because evidence lets an adult actually act, and telling means nobody handles it alone","new":"Evidence lets an adult act, and telling means nobody handles it alone"},
{"q":"The chat keeps going for days and it is getting worse. Why is screenshot and tell better than delete and hope?","old":"Because adults enjoy reading group chats","new":"Because a screenshot posted back into the chat shames the people doing it","fb":"Posting it back is fuel. The screenshot is for an adult, so they can act, and telling means nobody handles it alone."},
{"q":"The chat starts on Maya. You do not want to pile on, but leaving feels dramatic. What is the strongest first move?","old":"Post a long message telling everyone off","new":"Post a long message telling everyone off, so Maya can see you defended her","fb":"Maya sees one more message in the pile. No reaction, then a private message to check she is okay."},
{"q":"The chat starts on Maya. You do not want to pile on, but leaving feels dramatic. What is the strongest first move?","old":"Leave the chat instantly without a word","new":"Leave the chat instantly without a word, so you are not involved"},
{"q":"Why does the pile on need the audience more than it needs the first joke?","old":"Because audiences write better jokes","new":"Because the audience decides who the joke is about","fb":"The target was chosen by the first joke. Without reactions that joke dies. The audience is the fuel."},
{"q":"Which of these is an ending you install?","old":"I will stop when I feel tired","new":"I will stop when I feel tired, because your body knows better than a timer","fb":"Tired arrives long after the feed has won. An ending is installed before you press play."},
{"q":"Why did designers remove endings?","old":"Endings are old fashioned","new":"Because people complained that endings interrupted what they were enjoying","fb":"Nobody complained. An ending is a moment you might leave, and leaving is what the design is built to prevent."},
{"q":"Why did designers remove endings?","old":"To save money on making endings","new":"To save money on making endings, which cost extra to build"},
{"q":"Why does knowing about bottomless design actually change anything?","old":"Because seeing the design lets you stop blaming yourself and start installing your own endings","new":"Seeing the design lets you stop blaming yourself and install your own endings"},
{"q":"Why does knowing about bottomless design actually change anything?","old":"It does not, knowing changes nothing","new":"It does not, because knowing how a slot machine works never stopped anyone","fb":"Knowing is the first step, not the last. It lets you stop blaming yourself and install your own endings."},
{"q":"You want to actually stop after one episode tonight. Which plan works with your brain rather than against it?","old":"Rely on willpower in the moment","new":"Rely on willpower in the moment, because that is what strong people do","fb":"Strong people set things up so willpower is not needed. Autoplay off before you start makes stopping the default."},
{"q":"Scrolling before bed, every life looks better than yours and your chest goes tight. What is actually happening?","old":"The app is showing you the wrong people","new":"The app is showing you the wrong people, and following better accounts would fix it","fb":"Different accounts, same highlights. You are comparing your whole real life to a stack of chosen moments."},
{"q":"Scrolling before bed, every life looks better than yours and your chest goes tight. What is actually happening?","old":"Nothing, feeds have no effect on feelings","new":"Nothing, feeds have no effect on feelings, you are just tired"},
{"q":"What does a follower count actually measure?","old":"How many real friends someone has","new":"How many people would show up for someone if it mattered","fb":"Followers tap once and scroll on. The count is reach, not relationship, and showing up is a different number."},
{"q":"Who counts for more than any follower number?","old":"Whoever has the most followers themselves","new":"Whoever has the most followers, because thousands of people cannot all be wrong","fb":"Thousands of taps are not a judgement. The people who would show up when it matters count for more."},
{"q":"Why does nobody post their ordinary Tuesday?","old":"Ordinary Tuesdays are secret","new":"Because people who post ordinary days lose followers straight away","fb":"Nobody loses followers over a Tuesday. Feeds reward highlights, so the reel is all you ever see."},
{"q":"Why does nobody post their ordinary Tuesday?","old":"Because other people do not have ordinary days","new":"Because other people do not have ordinary days like yours"},
{"q":"Why can a fake post survive the closest inspection?","old":"Because nobody inspects posts closely","new":"Because a professional fake is technically perfect","fb":"Perfect is its job. The proof lives outside the post, which is why you check sideways."},
{"q":"Why does the sideways check beat examining the post really carefully?","old":"Because fakes are built to survive careful looking, but they cannot fake every other newsroom","new":"Fakes are built to survive careful looking, but cannot fake every other newsroom"},
{"q":"Why does the sideways check beat examining the post really carefully?","old":"Because reading the whole post takes too long","new":"Because reading the whole post takes far too long, and speed is what matters online","fb":"Speed is what the fake is counting on. Sideways is quick anyway: open a tab and see who else is reporting it."},
{"q":"You cannot verify the story anywhere. What happens to it?","old":"Share it with maybe not true written on it","new":"Share it with maybe not true written on it, so people can decide for themselves","fb":"A warning label still spreads it. Unverified stops with you."},
{"q":"You cannot verify the story anywhere. What happens to it?","old":"Wait an hour and share it anyway","new":"Wait an hour and share it anyway, in case it gets confirmed"},
{"q":"You run the sideways check on the transfer message. A search shows no real news organisation reporting it, just two accounts reposting the same screenshot. Verdict?","old":"True because the account has news in its name","new":"Probably true, two accounts reposting is two sources","fb":"Two reposts of one screenshot is one source, copied. No newsroom has it, so it is almost certainly false."},
{"q":"A text says: Your game account will be DELETED in 24 hours. Verify now with your password: [link]. What is the move?","old":"Reply asking if it is really the game company","new":"Reply asking if it is really the game company, because a scammer would not answer","fb":"Scammers answer, warmly and fast. Do not reply, open the real app yourself, and tell your grown up."},
{"q":"What makes go direct beat clicking the link, every single time?","old":"Clicking links is rude","new":"The link is fine as long as it looks right and the page has the real logo on it","fb":"Logos copy in seconds. The real app shows the truth, the link shows the fisher's copy."},
{"q":"What makes go direct beat clicking the link, every single time?","old":"Nothing, the link is faster","new":"Nothing, the link is faster and goes to the same place"},
{"q":"Your best friend messages: quick, send me the 6 digit code that just texted you, I need it for a surprise. What is really happening?","old":"Probably a stolen account trying to steal yours next, so no code, and check with your friend off the app","new":"A stolen account, probably. Send no code, and check with your friend off the app"},
{"q":"Your best friend messages: quick, send me the 6 digit code that just texted you, I need it for a surprise. What is really happening?","old":"Fine to send, codes expire quickly anyway","new":"Fine to send, because the code expires in minutes and only works once","fb":"Once is all a thief needs. That code is the key to your account, so send nothing and check with your friend off the app."},
{"q":"A free wallpaper app asks for your location and your contacts when it installs. Run the test.","old":"Allow both, apps know what they need","new":"Allow both, because a free app has to make money somehow and that is the deal","fb":"That is the deal only if you take it. Wallpapers need neither, so say no to both and the app still works."},
{"q":"Who actually reads their privacy settings?","old":"Everyone, it is the normal thing to do","new":"Everyone, because the app makes you read them before you can tap accept","fb":"The app makes you scroll, not read. Almost nobody reads them, which is exactly why checking puts you ahead."},
{"q":"Who actually reads their privacy settings?","old":"Only lawyers can understand them","new":"Only lawyers can understand them, so there is no point trying"},
{"q":"Why does a free wallpaper app want your contacts?","old":"To send wallpapers to your friends","new":"To let you send wallpapers to your friends, which you might want later","fb":"It could ask for that when you tap share. Your friends list is worth money, and the app does not need it to work."},
{"q":"Why is a free video app worth millions of pounds?","old":"Because what it learns about millions of viewers makes its adverts precisely aimed, and aimed adverts sell","new":"Precisely aimed adverts. What it learns about millions of viewers is what makes them sell"},
{"q":"Why is a free video app worth millions of pounds?","old":"Adverts alone, the same as posters at a bus stop","new":"Adverts alone, the same as posters at a bus stop, just with far more people walking past","fb":"A bus stop poster knows nothing about who walks past. This app knows, and knowing is what advertisers pay for."}
]$j$::jsonb)
where stage_id = 'explorer' and status = 'live' and slides is not null;
